## 3. SOURCE PRIORITY

Use only public, verifiable material.

### Tier 1 — Primary official evidence

Prioritize:

- central-bank speech pages;
- official transcripts;
- official interviews;
- official press conferences;
- testimony;
- hearing transcripts;
- official calendars;
- regional central-bank pages;
- official minutes where relevant.

Whenever possible, open the actual text.

### Tier 2 — High-quality reporting

Prioritize:

- Reuters;
- Bloomberg;
- Financial Times;
- Wall Street Journal;
- major national financial media;
- specialist central-bank reporters;
- credible local financial press.

### Tier 3 — Human interpretation

Search for:

- named economists;
- named strategists;
- former central bankers;
- respected central-bank journalists;
- public bank research;
- public research-house commentary;
- institutional interviews;
- credible market-desk analysis.

The purpose of Tier 3 is **not to replace the policymaker's words**.

Its purpose is to answer:

> How did informed humans interpret this communication?

===============================================================
## 4. MANDATORY WINDOW SWEEP

Before constructing the tape, perform a speaker sweep.

### 4.1 Build the relevant policymaker roster

Include the officials who could materially affect policy interpretation.

Adapt by institution.

Examples:

Fed:
- Chair;
- Vice Chair;
- Vice Chair for Supervision;
- Governors;
- New York Fed President;
- voting regional presidents;
- high-signal non-voters where relevant.

ECB:
- President;
- Vice President;
- Executive Board;
- major NCB governors;
- important hawkish / dovish voices.

BoE:
- Governor;
- Deputy Governors;
- MPC external members.

Other central banks:
- Governor;
- deputies;
- board / committee members;
- other identifiable high-signal policymakers.

Do not mechanically research every low-signal official if the institution has a very large roster.

Use policy relevance.

### 4.2 Search in three passes

#### PASS 1 — Official sweep

Check:

- speech page;
- official calendar;
- press releases;
- testimony;
- interviews;
- panels;
- minutes / policy documents where relevant.

#### PASS 2 — Media sweep

Search Reuters, Bloomberg and other high-quality reporting for:

- speeches not posted officially;
- interview remarks;
- panel Q&A;
- unscripted comments;
- committee-split reporting;
- clarification of ambiguous official remarks.

#### PASS 3 — Individual speaker check

For every important roster member, determine:

- spoke in-window;
- spoke but no monetary-policy content;
- no verified remarks found;
- possible speech / interview not cleanly captured.

===============================================================
## 5. SPEAKER DISCOVERY IS THE PRIMARY TASK

Do not stop after finding one or two major speeches.

The main failure to avoid is:

**missing a policy-relevant speaker.**

For every important official who spoke in-window, attempt to capture:

- date;
- venue;
- role / voting status where relevant;
- primary source;
- policy-relevant passages;
- short representative quote;
- whether the remarks materially differed from their prior position.

If the speech cannot be opened but reliable reporting captures it, say so explicitly.

If neither the primary text nor reliable reporting can be retrieved:

**Content gap — speech identified but policy substance not cleanly captured.**

Do not reconstruct the missing remarks.

===============================================================
## 6. PRIMARY-SOURCE EXTRACTION

For every material policymaker communication, extract only what is actually policy-relevant.

Possible topics include:

- next policy move;
- pace of easing or tightening;
- hold duration;
- terminal / neutral rate;
- inflation;
- labor market;
- growth;
- financial conditions;
- FX;
- balance sheet / QT;
- liquidity;
- political or institutional constraints;
- communication regime.

Do not force every category onto every speech.

For each speaker, identify:

### Key policy signal

What did the policymaker actually communicate?

### Increment versus previous stance

Did the speaker:

- change position;
- clarify an existing position;
- reinforce it;
- hedge it;
- contradict another policymaker;
- provide no meaningful new policy information?

Do not invent a change merely because the speaker spoke.

===============================================================
## 7. HUMAN VOICE — MANDATORY RESEARCH LAYER

After capturing the official remarks, search for credible human interpretation of the **speaker communication itself**.

This is a central purpose of the scout.

For important speeches, seek where available:

- Reuters journalist framing;
- Bloomberg journalist framing;
- named economists;
- named strategists;
- central-bank specialists;
- former policymakers;
- institutional research commentary.

### Human-voice questions

Look for answers to questions such as:

- What did professionals think was genuinely new?
- Was the speaker interpreted as hawkish, dovish or mainly clarifying?
- Did observers think the remarks altered expectations for the next meeting?
- Did they see the official as representing the committee center or a tail?
- Was an apparently strong statement discounted by professionals?
- Did different commentators interpret the remarks differently?
- Was the important signal in the prepared text or the Q&A?
- Did the speech resolve or deepen an existing committee disagreement?

### Do not force voice counts

For a major Chair / Governor / pivotal-member speech:

target approximately **2–4 genuinely useful human interpretations** when available.

For ordinary speakers:

**1–2 useful interpretations** may be enough.

These are research targets, not quotas.

An honest gap is preferable to invented richness.

If none are found:

**No independent readable human interpretation retrieved for this communication.**

===============================================================
## 8. HUMAN VOICE EVIDENCE RULES

A qualifying external voice must be identifiable.

Record:

- name;
- institution / role;
- outlet;
- date;
- whether it is a direct quote or paraphrase;
- what interpretation it uniquely contributes.

A journalist may provide valuable framing even when no economist is quoted.

Label this:

**Journalist framing**

rather than economist view.

Do not count:

- anonymous aggregators;
- AI summaries;
- copied news snippets;
- several syndications of the same Reuters article;
- the same economist repeated across multiple publications.

Preserve genuine disagreement.

Do not synthesize different human views into an artificial consensus.

===============================================================
## 9. QUOTE RULES

Direct quotes must be:

- visible in opened readable text;
- correctly attributed;
- short;
- policy relevant.

Normally:

- maximum one short policymaker quote per communication;
- maximum one short quote per external human voice.

Prefer paraphrase for longer arguments.

Use:

**Direct quote:** "[exact fragment]"

or:

**Paraphrase:** [accurate summary]

Never put a paraphrase inside quotation marks.

===============================================================
## 10. WINDOW DISCIPLINE

Every piece of evidence must be classified as:

### IN-WINDOW

Communication occurred inside the monitoring window.

Can drive the current speaker tape.

### CARRY-OVER

Earlier communication needed to understand a change or comparison.

Use sparingly.

### UNVERIFIED / GAP

Speech, comment or interpretation may exist but could not be cleanly verified.

Cannot drive conclusions.

### NON-POLICY

Communication was found but contained no material monetary-policy content.

Do not silently omit major officials.

===============================================================
## 11. WHAT THE SCOUT SHOULD NOT DO

Do NOT:

- rebuild macro release blocks;
- analyze CPI, payrolls, GDP, PMIs or other releases independently;
- produce a full macro regime judgment;
- create elaborate policy scenarios;
- create arbitrary inflation or labor thresholds;
- construct a full OIS probability tree;
- make FX or rates recommendations;
- write the final weekly macro note;
- create long AI-generated reaction-function essays;
- infer committee consensus from one loud speaker;
- turn every speech into a hawkish/dovish binary;
- invent human interpretation when readable commentary is absent.

The downstream agent owns synthesis.

